                <pb facs="0873" n="771" sameAs="frus:frus1933v03/pg771"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">711.922/47: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Siam (Baker) to the Acting Secretary of
                            State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Bangkok</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1933-11-24T15:00:00+07:00">November 24, 1933—3
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received November 24—7:20 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>28. Referring to Department’s telegram No. 17, November 18, 1 p.m. Legation has
                    no reason to believe injury to American interests will result from any of the
                    treaty changes suggested by the Siamese Government. Proposal relating to
                    monopolies apparently not aimed at American interests but to overcome rights
                    acquired from American treaty by most-favored-nation clause in other treaties.
                    Abolition of monopoly clause may possibly open up a field for future investment
                    of American capital.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Baker</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
